A||||9||Sevaan||Broken Key||||1||
Z||000000||Sevaan||06-17-2001||09:43 PM||||Does anyone know how to extract a broken key? There is one jammed into the lock on my passenger side door. How can I go about removing it without having to take apart the door?<P>Thanks,<BR>Sevaan<P>[Silver 1986 4-Cyl Sport Coupe]||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroFanaticus2||06-17-2001||09:53 PM||||Call a lock smith?  <BR>My uncle used to use something like a paperclip that had been bent into a curvey shape with a 'claw' like end to it.  He would push it past some of the pins or tumblers in the lock until he could feel the ridges on the key, then kind of gently scrape and tug on the key with the clip until it popped out.  But I am not a lock smith and it has been nearly 10 years since I have heard any of his techniques so this may or may not work.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||06-17-2001||10:46 PM||||Allot depends how bound up in the lock the key section is....<P>Dental picks with small hooked tips might get it.<P>If you can get it so you can see the end, and if the thing is not bound up in there... You might be able to take a section of wire, bent a small L on the end then super glue it to the key section. Warning, This is a one shot kind of deal... If you get sloppy with the glue or miss the key then you may never get it out.<P>Another slight posible. A variable speed dremel with a fine drill bit. Drill a hole into the key on an angle so you can hook something in the hole. Variable speed because it's easier to control and you're less likely to break a bit.||||reg||1
Z||000003||CJ||06-19-2001||09:12 AM||||Try a blade from a coping saw.  Break or cut the blade so a tooth is at the very end.  May be able to hook the key with that tooth.||||reg||1
Z||000004||DRH||06-19-2001||12:12 PM||||Really strong magnet?<P>Never tried it... Just a thought.<P><BR>||||reg||5
Z||000005||SteveJ||06-19-2001||12:28 PM||||Most keys are brass, but you knew that right?||||reg||1
Z||000006||DRH||06-19-2001||12:40 PM||||Oops...<P>To tell you the truth, I never thought about it. I guess that explains why they turn brass colored as they get worn, huh?<P>Cancel the magnet idea!!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>||||reg||11
Z||000007||fierobaby||06-19-2001||01:25 PM||||personally I like the magnet idea to bad it won't work! or you could call a lock smith... or jusy change all the locks in your car? I dunno just a thought never had to deal with that problem... I have the evil bent key that has a heck of a time going int o my driver side door I just hope it doesn't break <P>------------------<BR>1984 Sweet Candy Apple red Fiero sport coupe :-D||||reg||10
Z||000008||84Bill||06-19-2001||01:39 PM||||In the military we had to clean up all the "brass" shell casings before we left the range. I saw a sign that said "3,000,000 prize for the person that invents a brass magnet" I don't think that army post is still there but I think it's safe to say the brass magnet prize is still unclaimed.||||reg||1
Z||000009||beachbomb||06-19-2001||01:53 PM||||Hey sevann, i'm a locksmith,my dad use to own his own locksmith co. and i was certified through him, anyway, take it to a lock-smith near you, he'll have tools that should be able to pull the key out without damage to the lock, in rare cases you might need to replace the lock, but i doubt it....good luck..Jim(2 '84 Indys)||||reg||1
